1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,300
 What is a Dockerfile? It is a plain text document containing sets of instructions for Docker

2
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:17,620
 to build an image. This file will tell Docker how to build the image of our .NET app.

3
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:25,840
 Dockerfile builds image in layers with each instruction creating a new layer. Let us now

4
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:33,560
 understand the Dockerfile of our .NET app. There are stages and each starting from the

5
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:37,800
 from instruction.

6
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:54,800
 It gives each of these stage some named like base, build, publish and final.

7
00:00:56,680 --> 00:01:10,560
 See the first stage from mci.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet colon 10 references the .NET

8
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:20,640
 10 runtime image which is a lightweight compared to SDK version. This stage is given the name base.

9
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:33,040
 The dollar app underscore UID is an environment variable for non-root users that will be used to

10
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:43,200
 run the command here. The work directory slash app creates a directory by the name of app in the

11
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:52,240
 image of our .NET app. And it sets it as the working directory. It then exposes the container

12
00:01:52,240 --> 00:02:03,360
 port's number 8080 and 8081 to the outside world. That is to the host machine. So this way we can

13
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:12,160
 access the container on these ports. Obviously one port will be for HTTP and the other for the

14
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:21,280
 HTTPS versions. So we have our local machine on the left and it contains our .NET app with

15
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:30,560
 Dockerfile and on the right here is the Docker image of our app. The first stage tells .NET app

16
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:41,040
 image to reference the .NET runtime image from mci. It then creates a work directory called app

17
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:52,560
 inside the image and exposes ports 8080 and 8081 of the container that will be containing this

18
00:02:52,560 --> 00:03:01,280
 image of our app. Let's now move to the second stage. Here will be restoring and building the app

19
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:11,680
 using .NET restore and .NET build commands. So we have reference the SDK image of .NET 10 and

20
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:20,960
 not its runtime image like before. The name this stage build argument build underscore configuration

21
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:33,280
 is set to release. Then work directory slash src creates a new directory called src in the .NET

22
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:46,240
 apps image. Inside this directory first docker app cs project file is copied from the local machine.

23
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:55,280
 Now the .NET restore command is run for the cs project file which is inside the docker image

24
00:03:55,280 --> 00:04:06,400
 of the .NET app. This will restore the dependencies like NuGet packages and dls which the app has

25
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:18,560
 after that with copy .NET command all the apps files are copied from the local machine to the src

26
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:26,480
 directory on the image. The first dot is for the from location on the local machine

27
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:34,560
 and the second dot is for the location on the image which is set to the work dir instruction.

28
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:42,880
 Finally the .NET build command builds the apps cs project file inside the src directory.

29
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:53,200
 In the build command configuration is set as release and output from this command will be placed

30
00:04:53,200 --> 00:05:01,600
 in the apps slash build directory inside the image. Let us understand this from the animations.

31
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:10,400
 The src directory is first created inside the .NET apps image. Now the apps project file is copy

32
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:18,480
 to this src directory from the local machine. Next we run the .NET restore command on this cs

33
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:27,520
 project file. After that we copy all the apps files from the local machine to inside this src

34
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:35,680
 directory. Finally we build this app cs project file and the output is placed inside the apps

35
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:44,960
 slash build directory. Notice the dash O parameter given on the .NET build command. Moving to the

36
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:52,640
 third stage where we publish the app by running the .NET publish command we reference the build

37
00:05:52,640 --> 00:06:01,840
 stage from build as publish. The .NET publish command is run on the cs project file of the app which

38
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:11,680
 is residing on the src directory in the image. Build_configuration is kept as release and output

39
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:21,600
 is set to app slash publish folder inside the image of the .NET app. The use app host property

40
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:28,720
 is set to false. This will disable the creation of platform specific executable of the app.

41
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:36,560
 This means it will be producing platform independent exc of the app. Let's see the animations to

42
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:45,120
 understand this. We run .NET publish command on the cs project file located inside the src

43
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:52,880
 directory and the output of this command goes to app slash published directory inside the image.

44
00:06:53,520 --> 00:07:00,240
 Here the app published files are kept. Final stage references the base stage.

45
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:11,760
 Then work directory is set to app. After that the copy command is used to copy from publish stage

46
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:22,080
 app slash publish directory to the app directory on the route. Finally the entry point instruction

47
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:31,360
 specifies to run the dll called first docker app dot dll when the container starts.

48
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:38,800
 In this way whenever the container starts our .NET app will start running.

49
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:48,320
 So this is the slide of the final stage. It copies all the files from the app slash publish folder

50
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:57,440
 to the app folder and adds entry point to the first docker app dot dll file. What is dot docker

51
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:05,760
 ignore file? You will find it below the docker file. Click the arrow in front of the docker file

52
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:16,160
 to open the dot docker ignore file. This file lists the files and folders directories to exclude

53
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:25,760
 from the building context of the image. There are matching patterns given per line example

54
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:35,760
 asterisk asterisk means to match anything. So asterisk asterisk dot class path means to ignore files

55
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:45,120
 that are of dot class path extension. And residing on the local machine the exclamation pattern

56
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:52,560
 is to re include the files and directories that are excluded by the previous pattern.

57
00:08:53,360 --> 00:09:03,200
 So this means these patterns will be included. Example the dot get ignore file will be included

58
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:05,440
 similarly for the others.

